ecoded **against its own key**, and a payload whose mode, token scope, generation identity or segment epoch disagrees with the token naming it is likewise a typed error rather than an obligation that would reclaim one attempt's bytes while clearing another's. The owned staging entry `sidx:<id>:<n>:<chunk>` is the lease record a streaming write stores under `pending:<chunk>`, plus the owning upload id (`owner`) and the chunk's planned placement (`staged` — an erasure scheme the coder must support, and a D server per planned fragment, a count that decode leaves unchecked as for a committed chunk). Both are present on an owned entry and absent on an ordinary lease, and each is omitted when absent, so a `pending:` record written before them decodes and re-encodes byte-identically — load-bearing because a renewal puts a freshly encoded entry over the bytes it read. A value whose fields disagree with each other or with its key (one field alone, an owned entry under `pending:`, an ordinary lease under `sidx:`, an owner other than the key's) is refused by every reader and both `pending:` writers, and an expiry sweep skips it without reclaiming its chunk; GC's expired-lease input, off unless an operator arms it, also names it on the audit seam, and the post-restore reconciliation holds its chunk unmarked and reports the entry as needing a human, as it does an unreadable chunk map. An ownership field spelled `null` is refused too: the encoder never emits that spelling, so a lease that carried it would be rewritten by its next renewal. Their first production reader and writer is the post-restore session fence (`wyrd custodian --reconcile-after-restore`, `crates/custodian/src/restore.rs`): after its reference passes it re-lists `mpu:` in bounded pages and moves each `Open@E` session to `Aborting@E+1` in one commit with its `retire:` `{session, all}` obligation, so a session resurrected by the restore cannot be completed over reclaimed bytes (proposal 0016 D-B). `CreateMultipart